content.text:
Verified byte-identical — the proposed default is behaviour-preserving. Here's the change.

## 1. New: the seam itself

`src/Intropy.Framework.Blocks/Shared/ContractSerialization.cs`

```csharp
using System.Text.Json;
using CloudNative.CloudEvents;
using CloudNative.CloudEvents.SystemTextJson;

namespace Intropy.Framework.Blocks.Shared;

/// <summary>
/// How a component encodes and decodes the payload its messages carry.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// One object rather than loose options because the two halves must agree: the
/// formatter writes the payload on publish and the same options read it on
/// receive. A contract project generated from a registry schema registers its
/// own — its converters are what make a schema's string enums round-trip as the
/// schema spells them.
/// </remarks>
public sealed class ContractSerialization
{
    public ContractSerialization(JsonSerializerOptions options)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(options);
        Options = options;
        Formatter = new JsonEventFormatter(options, default);
    }

    /// <summary>The options the payload type is read and written with.</summary>
    public JsonSerializerOptions Options { get; }

    /// <summary>The CloudEvent formatter built from <see cref="Options"/>.</summary>
    public CloudEventFormatter Formatter { get; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What a component gets when nothing registers an encoding. Deliberately
    /// byte-identical to the previous hardcoded formatter:
    /// PropertyNameCaseInsensitive affects reads only, so published payloads are
    /// unchanged, while receive keeps the leniency the step bodies had inline.
    /// </summary>
    public static ContractSerialization Default { get; } =
        new(new JsonSerializerOptions { PropertyNameCaseInsensitive = true });
}
```

## 2. `DaprServiceInvoker.cs`

**Before** (lines 10-13, and 55):

```csharp
internal static class CloudEventSerializer
{
    internal static readonly JsonEventFormatter Formatter = new();
}

public class DaprServiceInvoker<TCtx>(
    DaprClient daprClient,
    HttpClient httpClient,
    string appId,
    Uri source,
    string type) : SendStep<TCtx> where TCtx : Context
{
    // ...
        var bytes = CloudEventSerializer.Formatter.EncodeStructuredModeMessage(input, out var contentType);
```

**After** — the static class is deleted outright:

```csharp
/// <param name="serialization">
/// How the payload is encoded. Null takes <see cref="ContractSerialization.Default"/>,
/// which is what every component got before this was configurable.
/// </param>
public class DaprServiceInvoker<TCtx>(
    DaprClient daprClient,
    HttpClient httpClient,
    string appId,
    Uri source,
    string type,
    ContractSerialization? serialization = null) : SendStep<TCtx> where TCtx : Context
{
    private readonly ContractSerialization _serialization = serialization ?? ContractSerialization.Default;

    // ...
        var bytes = _serialization.Formatter.EncodeStructuredModeMessage(input, out var contentType);
```

## 3. `DaprTopicPublisher.cs` — identical treatment

**Before** (line 46): `var bytes = CloudEventSerializer.Formatter.EncodeStructuredModeMessage(input, out _);`

**After**: same optional parameter, same `_serialization.Formatter`. These two are the only users of the deleted static.

## 4. The DI extension

```csharp
namespace Intropy.Framework.Blocks.Shared;

public static class ContractSerializationServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Registers how this component encodes its payload. A component that calls
    /// nothing keeps the default encoding.
    /// </summary>
    public static IServiceCollection AddContractSerialization(
        this IServiceCollection services, JsonSerializerOptions options) =>
        services.AddSingleton(new ContractSerialization(options));
}
```

Composition then changes from:

```csharp
services.AddSingleton<SendStep<Context>>(sp => new DaprTopicPublisher<Context>(
    sp.GetRequiredService<DaprClient>(),
    Constants.PubSubName, Constants.TopicName,
    new Uri(Constants.EventSource), Constants.EventType));
```

to the same call with `sp.GetService<ContractSerialization>()` appended — `GetService`, not `GetRequiredService`, so an unregistered component behaves exactly as today.

## 5. The test helpers must move together

`Intropy.Framework.Testing` decodes with its own formatters — `PublishedMessage.cs:30`, `DaprDelivery.cs:36`, `CapturedEnqueue.cs:27`, plus `EnqueueStep.cs:30` in transactional receive. If publish encoding becomes configurable and these don't, tests agree with each other and disagree with production, which is worse than the original bug. Each `new JsonEventFormatter()` becomes an optional `ContractSerialization? serialization = null` falling back to `Default`.

## What this does *not* touch

`LocalFileAdapter.cs:35`, `ExternalIdempotencyChecker.cs:109`, `CloudEventHelper.cs:27`, `ContextHelper.cs:30`. Those are internal wire formats where a payload contract's converters have no business. Scoping to payload encoding is the whole point.

## One wrinkle on the component side

quicktype emits `internal static class Converter`, so the component assembly **cannot** reach `Converter.Settings`. The Contracts project needs a small template-owned public accessor, in the same assembly:

```csharp
public static class ContractOptions
{
    public static JsonSerializerOptions Options => Converter.Settings;
}
```

Rendered only when a message pins a schema, under the same `spec.files` condition as the placeholder.

---

## A correction to the plan I wrote

Testing this turned up something that invalidates part of T2. **Two messages generated into the same namespace do not compile** — quicktype emits `Converter`, `IsoDateTimeOffsetConverter`, `DateOnlyConverter` and friends into *every* output file:

```
error CS0101: The namespace 'Contracts' already contains a definition for 'Converter'
```

Combining all schemas into one invocation fixes the collision but loses naming control: with multiple sources the schema's `title` wins over the filename, so `ProductUpdated.json` produced a root called `NewOrUpdatedProduct` and stuck `ProductUpdated` on a nested type. `--top-level` only governs a single-source run.

The fix that works, verified compiling: **one invocation per message into a per-message namespace** — `--namespace Contracts.ProductExport --top-level ExportEvent`. Each file is self-contained, each root is named exactly what `contract` says. This makes `system-host/skeleton/Topics.cs.tmpl`'s existing `contractNamespace` support load-bearing rather than optional, and the component's `using {{ .contracts }};` becomes the per-message namespace.

Want me to update T2 in the plan file with that, and fold this framework work in as its own unit?

